     <p><strong>General information</strong></p>
     <p>The following information will provide you with an easy to navigate overview of what will happen with your personal data when you visit our website. The term “personal data” comprises all data that can be used to personally identify you. For detailed information about the subject matter of data protection, please consult our Data Protection Declaration, which we have included beneath this copy.</p>
     
-    <h2>2. General information and mandatory information</h2>
-    <p><strong>Data protection</strong></p>
-    <p>The operators of this website and its pages take the protection of your personal data very seriously. Hence, we handle your personal data as confidential information and in compliance with the statutory data protection regulations and this Data Protection Declaration.</p>
-    <p>Whenever you use this website, a variety of personal information will be collected. Personal data comprises data that can be used to personally identify you. This Data Protection Declaration explains which data we collect as well as the purposes we use this data for. It also explains how, and for which purpose the information is collected.</p>
-    
+    <h2>2. Privacy by Design &amp; Data Minimization</h2>
+    <p><strong>Minimal data collection &amp; purpose limitation</strong></p>
+    <p>We follow the principle of data minimization (“if you don't have the data, you can't lose it”). Creating an account on The DevTools Times requires only a pseudonymous username and password—we do not ask for your legal name, email address, phone number, or date of birth. Authentication credentials are used strictly for verifying your identity and managing your reading preferences, and are never repurposed for marketing or profiling.</p>
+    <p>Sensitive authentication pages (<code>/signin</code> and <code>/signup</code>) do not load third-party analytics or advertising scripts, enforce <code>Cache-Control: no-store</code>, and strip outgoing <code>Referer</code> headers when communicating with authentication endpoints.</p>
+
     <h2>3. Recording of data on our website</h2>
-    <p><strong>Cookies</strong></p>
-    <p>In some instances, our website and its pages use so-called cookies. Cookies do not cause any damage to your computer and do not contain viruses. The purpose of cookies is to make our website more user friendly, effective and more secure. Cookies are small text files that are placed on your computer and stored by your browser.</p>
+    <p><strong>Cookies &amp; local storage</strong></p>
+    <p>Our website uses strictly necessary session cookies (with <code>HttpOnly</code> and <code>SameSite=Lax</code> protections) solely to store your reading list bookmarks during your visit. We never store sensitive tokens or personally identifiable information (PII) in browser <code>localStorage</code>.</p>
+    <p><strong>Third-party resources &amp; telemetry</strong></p>
+    <p>On content pages, external typography and anonymous click-interaction telemetry are loaded with <code>referrerpolicy="no-referrer"</code> and document-level <code>Referrer-Policy: strict-origin-when-cross-origin</code> and <code>Permissions-Policy</code> headers so that URL parameters, browsing history, and device sensors (camera, microphone, geolocation) are never exposed to third-party origins.</p>
+
+    <h2>4. Your Data Rights &amp; Account Erasure</h2>
+    <p>You have the right to access, correct, or delete any personal data associated with your account at any time. Signing out or deleting your account clears all session state (via <code>Clear-Site-Data</code>), ensuring no lingering credentials remain in the browser.</p>
 </LegalLayout>
